Dialogue: 0,0:00:02.00,0:00:05.00,Dialogue,,0,0,0,,Morning, Help Desk. Pip speaking.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:02.00,0:00:08.00,NoteL,speech/about these notes/high,0,0,0,,{\fad(250,350)}{\fs26\b1\c&HC8C8C8&}ABOUT THESE NOTES{\b0}{\fs35\c&HF0F0F0&}\NSample notes for an invented scene. Each note appears just after its line. “(less sure)” means: check me.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.50,0:00:09.50,Dialogue,,0,0,0,,Pip, the Wi-Fi's gone down again and I've got a call\Nwith Head Office in five minutes.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:10.00,0:00:14.00,Dialogue,,0,0,0,,Have you tried walking round the office\Nholding the router above your head?
Dialogue: 1,0:00:12.00,0:00:22.67,NoteR,visual/visual gag/high,0,0,0,,{\fad(250,350)}{\fs26\b1\c&H50B4FF&}VISUAL GAG{\b0}{\fs35\c&HF0F0F0&}\NNotes about what is on screen carry a video time instead of a quote. This is where one would describe a prop or a sight gag.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:14.50,0:00:17.50,Dialogue,,0,0,0,,Is that a real fix, or are you taking the mickey?
Dialogue: 1,0:00:17.70,0:00:26.37,NoteL,speech/british english/high,0,0,0,,{\fad(250,350)}{\fs26\b1\c&H5AC8FF&}BRITISH ENGLISH{\b0}{\fs35\c&HF0F0F0&}\NTo “take the mickey” (or “take the mick”) is to tease or make fun of someone. Mildly cheeky, never harsh.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:18.00,0:00:19.50,Dialogue,,0,0,0,,Bit of both.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:19.70,0:00:29.03,NoteL,speech/delivery/high,0,0,0,,{\fad(250,350)}{\fs26\b1\c&HFFE08C&}DELIVERY{\b0}{\fs35\c&HF0F0F0&}\NThe deadpan is the joke: Pip admits the “fix” is half genuine, half a wind-up. Understatement is a staple of British comedy.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:21.00,0:00:25.00,Dialogue,,0,0,0,,I've just been to the canteen.\NThere's not one custard cream left.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:25.20,0:00:33.87,NoteL,speech/cultural reference/high,0,0,0,,{\fad(250,350)}{\fs26\b1\c&HFFB45A&}CULTURAL REFERENCE{\b0}{\fs35\c&HF0F0F0&}\NA custard cream is a classic British biscuit (a cookie, to Americans): two vanilla biscuits sandwiching a sweet cream filling.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:25.50,0:00:28.00,Dialogue,,0,0,0,,That's a priority one incident, Marcus.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:28.20,0:00:36.87,NoteL,speech/it in-joke/high,0,0,0,,{\fad(250,350)}{\fs26\b1\c&H7CE07C&}IT IN-JOKE{\b0}{\fs35\c&HF0F0F0&}\NIn IT support, “P1” is the highest urgency, normally reserved for outages. Treating missing biscuits as one is the gag.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:28.50,0:00:30.00,Dialogue,,0,0,0,,I'm gutted.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:30.20,0:00:36.87,NoteL,speech/british english/high,0,0,0,,{\fad(250,350)}{\fs26\b1\c&H5AC8FF&}BRITISH ENGLISH{\b0}{\fs35\c&HF0F0F0&}\N“Gutted” means bitterly disappointed. It's casual and often exaggerated for comic effect, as here.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:31.50,0:00:35.00,Dialogue,,0,0,0,,Never mind the biscuits. I'll give you a fiver\Nif the Wi-Fi's back by quarter past.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:35.20,0:00:42.20,NoteL,speech/british english/high,0,0,0,,{\fad(250,350)}{\fs26\b1\c&H5AC8FF&}BRITISH ENGLISH{\b0}{\fs35\c&HF0F0F0&}\NA fiver is a five-pound note; a tenner is ten pounds. Both are everyday slang.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:35.50,0:00:39.00,Dialogue,,0,0,0,,Make it a tenner and a bacon butty\Nand you've got yourself a deal.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:39.20,0:00:49.87,NoteL,speech/british english/medium,0,0,0,,{\fad(250,350)}{\fs26\b1\c&H5AC8FF&}BRITISH ENGLISH{\b0}  {\c&H8C8CFF&}(less sure){\c&HF0F0F0&}{\fs35\c&HF0F0F0&}\NA “butty” is a sandwich, mostly in the North of England. A bacon butty is a bread roll with bacon, often with ketchup or brown sauce.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:42.00,0:00:46.00,Dialogue,,0,0,0,,Right. It was the cleaner.\NShe unplugged the router to hoover.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:46.50,0:00:48.50,Dialogue,,0,0,0,,Typical. Absolutely typical.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:49.50,0:00:52.50,Dialogue,,0,0,0,,Pop the kettle on, Marcus. We've earned it.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:52.70,0:01:00.37,NoteL,speech/cultural reference/high,0,0,0,,{\fad(250,350)}{\fs26\b1\c&HFFB45A&}CULTURAL REFERENCE{\b0}{\fs35\c&HF0F0F0&}\NPutting the kettle on for tea is the British answer to nearly every situation, good or bad.
